\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Gibbs energy Hessian analysis for four BCC\textsubscript  {A2} compositions at $T = \SI {500}{\celsius  }$. Diagonal elements $H_i \equiv \partial ^{2}G^\alpha _m/\partial x_i^{2}$ are evaluated at the nominal composition; \textbf  {negative values (bold)} indicate that the element is locally spinodally unstable and drives decomposition, while large positive values act as numerical stiffeners. $\lambda _{\min }$ is the minimum eigenvalue of the full Hessian matrix (negative = globally spinodal); $H_{\max }$ is the largest eigenvalue (the stiffness driver). $\Delta t$ is the stability-limited time step (Eq.~\ref {eq:dt_stability}); $\tau $ is the spinodal growth timescale at the dominant wavenumber; $t_{50k}$ is the physical time reached in 50\,000 steps; and $t_{50k}/\tau $ is the number of growth timescales completed — a direct measure of how much microstructural evolution has occurred. Simulation parameters: $256\times 256$ grid, $L = \SI {1}{\micro  \metre }$, $M = \SI {5e-21}{\metre \cubed \second \per \kg }$. }}{19}{table.caption.71}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hessian_comparison}{{3}{19}{Gibbs energy Hessian analysis for four BCC\textsubscript {A2} compositions at $T = \SI {500}{\celsius }$. Diagonal elements $H_i \equiv \partial ^{2}G^\alpha _m/\partial x_i^{2}$ are evaluated at the nominal composition; \textbf {negative values (bold)} indicate that the element is locally spinodally unstable and drives decomposition, while large positive values act as numerical stiffeners. $\lambda _{\min }$ is the minimum eigenvalue of the full Hessian matrix (negative = globally spinodal); $H_{\max }$ is the largest eigenvalue (the stiffness driver). $\Delta t$ is the stability-limited time step (Eq.~\ref {eq:dt_stability}); $\tau $ is the spinodal growth timescale at the dominant wavenumber; $t_{50k}$ is the physical time reached in 50\,000 steps; and $t_{50k}/\tau $ is the number of growth timescales completed — a direct measure of how much microstructural evolution has occurred. Simulation parameters: $256\times 256$ grid, $L = \SI {1}{\micro \metre }$, $M = \SI {5e-21}{\metre \cubed \second \per \kg }$}{table.caption.71}{}}
\newlabel{tab:hessian_comparison@cref}{{[table][3][]3}{[1][18][]19}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Composition fields from Cahn-Hilliard simulations (Jansson mode) of three BCC\textsubscript  {A2} alloys at $T = \SI {500}{\celsius  }$ ($256\times 256$ grid, $L = \SI {1}{\micro  \metre }$, $\kappa = \SI {1e-12}{\joule \per \metre }$, $M = \SI {5e-21}{\metre \cubed \second \per \kg }$). (a)~Binary Nb$_{50}$V$_{50}$: $x_{\rm  V}$ field after $t = \SI {800}{\second }$ (80\,000 steps, $\Delta t = \SI {0.01}{\second }$). Well-coarsened Nb-rich (blue) and V-rich (red) spinodal domains of characteristic size $\sim \SI {200}{\nano \metre }$ are visible. (b)~Ternary Nb$_{50}$Hf$_{10}$V$_{40}$: $x_{\rm  Nb}$ field after $t = \SI {51}{\second }$ (30\,000 steps, $\Delta t = \SI {1.71e-3}{\second }$). Both Nb and Hf are spinodally unstable ($H_{\rm  Nb},\,H_{\rm  Hf} < 0$), so there are no numerical stiffeners; the simulation completes $\gg 10^{3}\,\tau $ of evolution and clear fine-scale decomposition is observed ($x_{\rm  Nb}$ spans $[0.36,\,0.69]$). (c)~Quaternary Nb$_{50}$Hf$_{5}$Ti$_{5}$V$_{40}$: $x_{\rm  Nb}$ field after $t = \SI {40}{\second }$ (30\,000 steps, $\Delta t = \SI {1.32e-3}{\second }$). Although globally spinodally unstable ($\lambda _{\min } = \SI {-14811}{\joule \per \mol }$), Hf and Ti act as numerical stiffeners ($H_{\rm  Hf} = +\SI {255}{\kilo \joule \per \mol }$, $H_{\rm  Ti} = +\SI {365}{\kilo \joule \per \mol }$), forcing a ten-fold reduction in $\Delta t$ and limiting the simulation to only ${\approx }0.8\,\tau $ — far short of the $\gtrsim 10^{2}\,\tau $ required for visible domain formation. The composition field remains essentially uniform ($x_{\rm  Nb}$ spans only $[0.497,\,0.503]$). }}{19}{figure.caption.72}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:stiffness_microstructures}{{6}{19}{Composition fields from Cahn-Hilliard simulations (Jansson mode) of three BCC\textsubscript {A2} alloys at $T = \SI {500}{\celsius }$ ($256\times 256$ grid, $L = \SI {1}{\micro \metre }$, $\kappa = \SI {1e-12}{\joule \per \metre }$, $M = \SI {5e-21}{\metre \cubed \second \per \kg }$). (a)~Binary Nb$_{50}$V$_{50}$: $x_{\rm V}$ field after $t = \SI {800}{\second }$ (80\,000 steps, $\Delta t = \SI {0.01}{\second }$). Well-coarsened Nb-rich (blue) and V-rich (red) spinodal domains of characteristic size $\sim \SI {200}{\nano \metre }$ are visible. (b)~Ternary Nb$_{50}$Hf$_{10}$V$_{40}$: $x_{\rm Nb}$ field after $t = \SI {51}{\second }$ (30\,000 steps, $\Delta t = \SI {1.71e-3}{\second }$). Both Nb and Hf are spinodally unstable ($H_{\rm Nb},\,H_{\rm Hf} < 0$), so there are no numerical stiffeners; the simulation completes $\gg 10^{3}\,\tau $ of evolution and clear fine-scale decomposition is observed ($x_{\rm Nb}$ spans $[0.36,\,0.69]$). (c)~Quaternary Nb$_{50}$Hf$_{5}$Ti$_{5}$V$_{40}$: $x_{\rm Nb}$ field after $t = \SI {40}{\second }$ (30\,000 steps, $\Delta t = \SI {1.32e-3}{\second }$). Although globally spinodally unstable ($\lambda _{\min } = \SI {-14811}{\joule \per \mol }$), Hf and Ti act as numerical stiffeners ($H_{\rm Hf} = +\SI {255}{\kilo \joule \per \mol }$, $H_{\rm Ti} = +\SI {365}{\kilo \joule \per \mol }$), forcing a ten-fold reduction in $\Delta t$ and limiting the simulation to only ${\approx }0.8\,\tau $ — far short of the $\gtrsim 10^{2}\,\tau $ required for visible domain formation. The composition field remains essentially uniform ($x_{\rm Nb}$ spans only $[0.497,\,0.503]$)}{figure.caption.72}{}}
